<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scotch-Irish Americans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Scots-Irish Americans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scotch-Irish, or Scots-Irish, are Americans descended from Presbyterian settlers from Ulster, most of them of Scottish origin, who emigrated in large numbers in the 18th century. Many landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and moved into the backcountry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and the Carolinas, where they settled the frontier. They strongly shaped the culture of Appalachia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scotch-irish-americans.jpg" alt="Scotch-Irish Americans" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lightandtruth · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Scotch-Irish_Americans_in_2023.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ancestors of the Scotch-Irish had moved to Ulster in the 17th century, mainly from the long-troubled border counties of the Scottish Lowlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-england">Northern England</a>. Many settlers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a> belonged to dissenting Calvinist groups, chiefly Lowland Scottish Presbyterians but also English Puritans, Flemish Calvinists, French Huguenots and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-palatines">German Palatines</a>, who stood together against their Catholic Irish and Anglican English neighbours. The enforcement of Queen Anne's Popery Act of 1704, which discriminated against all outside the established church, drove considerable numbers to emigrate. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland people of the same descent call themselves Ulster Scots.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1710 to 1775 over 200,000 people went from Ulster to the thirteen colonies, most of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. Arriving late, they found the coastal land owned or too dear and moved inland, where the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-jackson-turner">Frederick Jackson Turner</a> called them the cutting-edge of the frontier. They followed the Susquehanna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> valleys, built log cabins, grist mills and Presbyterian churches, and founded towns such as Chambersburg, Gettysburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, while others went south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, the Carolinas and the Appalachian region.</p><p>On the frontier they bore much of the fighting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontiac">Pontiac</a>'s War. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, whose Quaker leaders had raised no militia, many of their settlements were destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, and in July 1763 the Assembly authorised a 700-strong militia whose rangers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paxton-boys">Paxton Boys</a> among them, soon raided Lenape villages. In the 1770s the Scotch-Irish generally supported independence, and Mecklenburg County, North Carolina, issued the Mecklenburg Declaration of 1775.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Presbyterianism defined the community. In 1746 Scotch-Irish Presbyterians founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeton-university">College of New Jersey</a>, later Princeton University, to train New Light ministers. After 1783 newcomers settled in Pittsburgh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> as skilled workers, and Pittsburgh's iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> leaders were, in one historian's judgement, largely Scotch-Irish. Many Scotch-Irish Americans also held enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>.</p><p>Their folk culture is clearest in Appalachia. Cecil Sharp, who collected hundreds of songs there, found the tunes closer to those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-england">Northern England</a> and the Lowlands than to the Gaelic Highlands, and settlers brought Ulster's hall-parlor house plan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> and Kentucky. Andrew Jackson, James Buchanan and Chester A. Arthur each had a parent born in Ulster.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Scotch-Irish Americans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Scots-Irish</b> and German settlers filled the backcountry.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-north-carolina">Province of North Carolina</a></li><li><blockquote>Quakers, Germans, beginning with Germantown in 1683, and later <b>Scots-Irish</b> settlers poured in, and grain exports made Philadelphia prosperous.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-pennsylvania">Province of Pennsylvania</a></li></ul></section>
